(3182918, 442730, 57358, '<td valign="top">hmm, I''m not familiar with the program you''re using, but it looks like you figured out the sizing thing.<br/> <br/>as for the photo, I think it would have faired better if you had put the focus on the front side end of the closest battery instead of the left-side one.  DPC likes a really sharp focus point, maybe try a sharpening tool.<br/> <br/>another suggestion I might have for you would be to use a colored piece of paper as a background to give this shot more interest.<br/> <br/>I really like the shallow depth of field and the composition that you used; this was a hard challenge to do for your first time<br/> <br/>As a general piece of advice for a newbie, DPC seems to like very colorful, very sharp images...<br/> <br/>Anyhow, a 4.6 is not a bad score for your first shot, this is a really tough crowd and if you take everything here with a grain of salt, you''ll be a pro in no time!</td>', 'hmm, I''m not familiar with the program you''re using, but it looks like you figured out the sizing thing. as for the photo, I think it would have faired better if you had put the focus on the front side end of the closest battery instead of the left-side one.  DPC likes a really sharp focus point, maybe try a sharpening tool. another suggestion I might have for you would be to use a colored piece of paper as a background to give this shot more interest. I really like the shallow depth of field and the composition that you used; this was a hard challenge to do for your first time As a general piece of advice for a newbie, DPC seems to like very colorful, very sharp images... Anyhow, a 4.6 is not a bad score for your first shot, this is a really tough crowd and if you take everything here with a grain of salt, you''ll be a pro in no time!', '2007-01-04 21:29:51.000000', NULL, 0),
